# Dispatchers

The always-on machines that turn a label or a reaction into a headless run. One row per machine; a repo reaches a dispatcher only through the group it is registered in.

| dispatcher | group | repos | owner | caps | notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| {{dispatcher}} | {{group}} | {{owner/repo}} | {{owner}} | {{blank, or e.g. runs 10 · step 72h · poll 1m · retry 15m · park 3}} | {{blank, or what this machine is for}} |

The `caps` cell says what a machine may do: `runs <n>` at once, `step <n>h` or `<n>m` before a run is stopped, `poll <n>m` or `<n>s` between passes, `retry <n>m` after a failure and `park <n>` failures before a person is asked. Each field takes only the units that field is measured in, because `poll 2h` and `step 10s` are neither of them a limit anybody means. Every field is optional and falls back to the shipped default; the header names the columns, so a cell is read by what its column is called rather than where it sits, and a cell that cannot be read refuses that machine by name rather than running it on numbers nobody chose. The caps belong to the machine — its processor, its subscription — which is why they sit here and not in a repo's dev.md, and they are re-read every pass, so a change lands on the next poll without a restart.

A repo with no row here runs only what an operator starts, and `dispatch:` stays `off` in its dev.md — `dispatch: local` is a machine-local opt-in a repo makes for itself, never something this file turns on. Provisioning the machine is `onboarding/dispatcher-box.md`.
